      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight Terrell lifts with the 20-pound weights.\n2. Determine the number of lifts required with the 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights.**\n\nTerrell lifts two 20-pound weights 12 times. The total weight lifted per lift is:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} = 40 \\text{ pounds/lift} \\]\n\nThe total weight lifted in 12 lifts is:\n\\[ 40 \\text{ pounds/lift} \\times 12 \\text{ lifts} = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the number of lifts required with the 15-pound weights.**\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights. The total weight lifted with 15-pound weights per lift is:\n\\[ 15 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 2 \\text{ weights} = 30 \\text{ pounds/lift} \\]\n\nWe need the total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights to be equal to 480 pounds. Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\\[ 30 \\text{ pounds/lift} \\times x \\text{ lifts} = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{480 \\text{ pounds}}{30 \\text{ pounds/lift}} = 16 \\text{ lifts} \\]\n\nThus, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights.",
