      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with two 20-pound weights, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight Terrell lifts with two 20-pound weights.\n2. Determine the weight lifted per lift with two 15-pound weights.\n3. Calculate the number of lifts required to achieve the same total weight.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights.**\n\nTerrell lifts two 20-pound weights 12 times. Therefore, the total weight lifted is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 20 \\times 12 = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the weight lifted per lift with two 15-pound weights.**\n\nWe need to find out how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights to lift a total of 480 pounds. Let \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell lifts two 15-pound weights. The total weight lifted with two 15-pound weights \\( x \\) times is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 15 \\times x = 30x \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nWe set this equal to the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights:\n\\[\n30x = 480\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Solve for \\( x \\).**\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 30:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{480}{30} = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
